;; DRIVER-LL Phase 4, sub-phases 4a and 4b: the sequencer.
;;
;; The stage loop of scripts/rfc_to_implementation.py:1915-2015 ported as
;; def-shell orchestration around three proved cores: skip.may-skip decides
;; resumption, stage.record-outcome classifies a halt, and (4b)
;; validate.verdict-of / validate.verdict-outcome decide and classify a
;; delegated output. None of the three is re-implemented here, which is Phase
;; 3's standing rule (spine.llmll:12-16).
;;
;; THIRTEEN OF THE SIXTEEN STAGES HAVE NO BODY. Proposal section 9's 4a row
;; held all sixteen at a stub; the 4b row lands B, C and I. A stage that is
;; not ported writes a stub byte-string to each artifact it declares and
;; nothing else; the eleven resume and outcome transitions of section 2.3 are
;; decided over those stubs exactly as they would be over real artifacts,
;; because the resume gate reads digests and a completion record and never the
;; content. registry.stage-ported? is the switch and carries the retirement
;; schedule.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; SECTION 7 STATEMENT 3 OF 3 [DISCLOSURE validate.verdict-of], NEW AT 4b
;;
;; verdict-of is proved over `present: bool`, `size: int` and `floor: int`.
;; The floor is a registry constant a reader can check against the reference's
;; require() call. The other two are computed HERE, in unproved shell code,
;; from ONE wasi.fs.read of the declared output, and three decisions inside
;; that computation are invisible at the proved boundary:
;;
;;   1. PRESENCE AND DECODABILITY ARE COLLAPSED. wasi.fs.read opens with a
;;      UTF-8 encoding and yields RErr both for a path that does not exist and
;;      for one whose bytes are not valid UTF-8 (CodegenHs.hs wasi_fs_read).
;;      Every RErr is read as `present = false`, so an output that exists and
;;      cannot be decoded records the reference's "wrote no <file>" detail
;;      rather than a decoding one. The DECISION is the same under both -- the
;;      stage records `failed` either way, and section 7:283-286 wants exactly
;;      that of an output not meeting its declared shape -- so this costs the
;;      reason string and not the status. Distinguishing them needs a second
;;      command (wasi.fs.sha256 reads bytes and would answer RText), and that
;;      arm is not spent here.
;;
;;   2. `size` IS CODE POINTS, NOT BYTES. The reference tests
;;      `out.stat().st_size`, which is bytes; the port tests string-length of
;;      the decoded text. They agree on ASCII and disagree on any multi-byte
;;      character, in the direction of the port measuring SMALLER, so a
;;      non-ASCII scope.md of 201 bytes can fail a floor the reference passes.
;;      There is no wasi.fs.stat and FS-STAT-1 is filed for the mtime half of
;;      the same gap; a size accessor would close this one.
;;
;;   3. A RESPONSE ARM THAT IS NEITHER RText NOR RErr is read as absent, on
;;      the same reasoning as the 4a statement's item 4: no wasi.fs.read
;;      produces those arms, so the branch is unreachable rather than untaken,
;;      and it fails in the direction that forces a re-run.
;;
;; The three AgentRunner conditions (:221 timeout, :227 non-zero exit, :230
;; declared output absent) do NOT go through verdict-of. A budget overrun is
;; not a fact about an output's shape, and forcing it into a Verdict would be
;; a category error; those two construct Errored directly and reach
;; stage.record-outcome, whose [S4-FAILED] and [S4-NOT-STOPPED] are the proved
;; mapping. The THIRD of them, an absent declared output, does go through
;; verdict-of as `Absent`, because it is a fact about the output.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; THREE UNGUARDED READS IN THE REFERENCE, PORTED AS DECISIONS.
;;
;; Proposal section 9.1 item 1 settles two: read_json on PROVENANCE.json in
;; stage B (:573) and read_text on stage B's scope.md in stage I (:1062).
;; Both traceback out at exit 1 in the reference. The port HAS NO HOST
;; EXCEPTION TO FALL THROUGH, so it could not reproduce that even if that were
;; wanted; both are guarded and record `failed`/`Errored` at exit 3. Neither
;; is a MUST over artifact content identifiable by clause, so section 3.5's
;; rule gives `failed` directly. read_manifest set the precedent at 4a.
;;
;; A THIRD, WHICH THE CENSUS BEHIND SECTION 9.1 DID NOT NAME:
;; `(PROMPTS / name).read_text(encoding="utf-8")` in Ctx.prompt (:536). It is
;; shared by every delegated stage rather than owned by one, which is likely
;; why a per-stage census missed it. Same disposition, same reasoning, and it
;; is recorded here as a finding against section 9.1's enumeration rather than
;; folded in silently. A fourth sits at :625, `f.read_text(errors="replace")`
;; inside _sources_text; that one cannot raise on a decode error because of
;; the replace, so it is unguarded and also unreachable in the reference,
;; while the port's read of the same file yields RErr on undecodable bytes and
;; records `failed`. Divergence, disclosed, and it fails toward re-running.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; 4b CONSTRUCTS NO `stopped` SITE. Proposal section 9.1 item 3. Every
;; reachable halt in B, C and I records `failed`/`Errored`, and validate's
;; [V7-NO-STOP], [V7-NO-PARTIAL] and [V7-ONLY-TWO] make that a proof rather
;; than a convention over the delegated-output path. The 4a fault injector
;; still constructs ConditionUnmet and PartialThenHalt on the --halt-kind
;; flag; that is 4a scaffolding with a retirement schedule and is not a 4b
;; stage-body site.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; SECTION 7 STATEMENT 1 OF 2 [DISCLOSURE skip.may-skip]
;;
;; may-skip is proved over four booleans. Three of the four are computed HERE,
;; in unproved shell code, and the proof says nothing about whether they are
;; the right booleans. Four shell-side decisions, one more than proposal Rev 7
;; lists:
;;
;;   1. A recorded digest of None arrives as "" (manifest.recorded-digest) and
;;      compares unequal to every hex string, so an artifact whose integrity
;;      was never recorded is a MISMATCH. Mirrors the reference at :1941.
;;
;;   2. An unknown `status` string parses to manifest-complete = false. That is
;;      conformant with driver-spec section 5:189-191 and it is a shell reading
;;      of a spec sentence, not a proved mapping.
;;
;;   3. artifacts-present and digests-match are BOTH derived from one
;;      wasi.fs.sha256 per declared artifact. MEASURED at v0.14.84: sha256 on a
;;      missing path yields RErr and on a present one RText hex, so presence and
;;      digest collapse into one command distinguished by CONSTRUCTOR. Every
;;      RErr is read as "absent", so an unreadable but present artifact is
;;      indistinguishable from a missing one at the proved boundary. The
;;      collapse is conservative: it can only force a re-run, never permit a
;;      skip, so it cannot violate [S5-SKIP]. It violates no clause and can
;;      still be wrong, which is why it is disclosed rather than argued away.
;;
;;   4. A response arm that is neither RText nor RErr (RNone, RCode, RList)
;;      is also read as "absent". No wasi.fs.sha256 produces those arms, so the
;;      branch is unreachable rather than merely untaken, and it fails in the
;;      same safe direction.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; SECTION 7 STATEMENT 2 OF 2 [DISCLOSURE stage.record-outcome]
;;
;; record-outcome proves Outcome -> Status over all four constructors. WHICH
;; constructor a site builds is chosen here, in unproved shell code, and it is
;; the discrimination the Python driver lost (proposal section 3.4).
;;
;; At 4a the choice is FULLY ENUMERABLE and narrower than what 4b onward will
;; owe: two flags, --halt-at naming a stage key and --halt-kind naming a
;; constructor, and nothing else in this module constructs a halt. There is no
;; 46-site table to audit yet because there are no stage bodies. When the
;; bodies land, this statement widens to proposal section 3.5's rule plus
;; section 3.6's artifact-state axis, and the gap inventory must not read as
;; though 4a had closed something it deferred.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; THE FAULT INJECTOR, AND WHY 4a NEEDS ONE.
;;
;; Section 9 demands all four Outcome arms with no stage bodies. With no
;; bodies only Finished has a producer, so ConditionUnmet, Errored and
;; PartialThenHalt need a declared injector, which is this port's counterpart
;; to the rig's STUB_MODE. It is 4a-ONLY SCAFFOLDING and its retirement is
;; scheduled: 4b retires Errored (a delegated output absent or malformed), 4c
;; through 4e retire ConditionUnmet at the gates, and 4f retires
;; PartialThenHalt at stage O's perturbation-omission check per section 6.2.
;; The injected halt's `clause` is "4a-injector" and is NOT a driver-spec
;; clause; a real clause identifier arrives with the stage that owns it.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; THE PROCESS BOUNDARY (PROC-BOUNDARY-1, shipped v0.14.84).
;;
;; argv arrives as :init's command on the RList arm through RC-3, and the exit
;; status is the :status projection. Both halves are used as specified. One
;; measured LIMIT, and it is a finding against the proposal's section 4.4
;; rather than a defect:
;;
;;   the range refinement {v : int | 0 <= v <= 255} is proved only where the
;;   function :status names is BODY-FAITHFUL, and a projection out of a
;;   structured state is not. MEASURED at v0.14.84: a def whose body applies
;;   `first` to a pair falls back, for (int, int) as much as for this state
;;   type, so drv-status is contract-checked rather than proved. The
;;   proc_boundary.llmll fixture takes `[s: int]` and is the provable case; no
;;   driver with a product state is.
;;
;; What carries the weight instead is exit-code below, which IS body-faithful
;; and whose [EXIT-NOVACUOUS] post is the eliminative one: a run that did not
;; complete cannot exit 0. drv-status then clamps, so a state carrying 256
;; exits 255 rather than exiting 0 and reporting success.
;;
;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;; TWO OUT-OF-STAGE HALTS CARRY NO STAGE STATUS.
;;
;; A corrupt MANIFEST.json (proposal section 10 cases 16 to 18) is decided
;; before any stage exists, so no Status attaches to it and record-outcome is
;; not called. The exit code is the literal 2, mirroring the reference's
;; top-level `except Halt` handler (rfc_to_implementation.py:2027-2032), and
;; the manifest is NOT written. Calling exit-code with a manufactured Stopped
;; would assert a stage disposition that driver-spec section 4 does not reach.

(module sequencer
  (import wasi.io   (capability stdout :deterministic false))
  ;; CAP-1-REAL: the verb and the path are documentation, not a bound.
  (import wasi.fs   (capability read-write "/"))
  ;; wasi.proc.args shares the wasi.proc namespace with wasi.proc.run
  ;; (extractWasiNamespace takes the first two segments), so this clause grants
  ;; the argument vector.
  (import wasi.proc (capability exec :deterministic false))

  (import stage)    (open stage)
  (import skip)     (open skip)
  (import registry) (open registry)
  (import manifest) (open manifest)
  (import validate) (open validate)

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; The proved centre this module adds
  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------

  ;; The process exit status of a run whose disposition is `s`. Body-faithful,
  ;; so the range and the three mappings are proved rather than asserted.
  ;;
  ;; 0 / 2 / 3 mirror the reference's three return paths (:1978, :1994, :2015
  ;; and the final :2019). Pending is 1 and is unreachable: nothing here builds
  ;; Pending, and [S4-ATTEMPTED] forbids record-outcome from returning it.
  (def exit-code [s: Status] -> int

    (post (and (>= result 0) (<= result 255))
      :source "[EXIT-RANGE] PROC-BOUNDARY-1 sec 4.4 - a status outside 0..255 is truncated by POSIX, so 256 would exit 0 and report success")

    (post (=> (= s Complete) (= result 0))
      :source "[EXIT-COMPLETE] a run whose stages all completed exits 0")

    (post (=> (= s Stopped) (= result 2))
      :source "[EXIT-STOPPED] driver-spec.txt sec 4 - a stage that halted on a defined condition exits 2, as rfc_to_implementation.py:1978 does")

    (post (=> (= s Failed) (= result 3))
      :source "[EXIT-FAILED] driver-spec.txt sec 4 - a stage that halted for any other reason exits 3, as rfc_to_implementation.py:1994 does")

    ;; The eliminative one. A body that returned 0 for a halted run passes
    ;; every implication above that does not mention it and is refuted here.
    (post (=> (not (= s Complete)) (> result 0))
      :source "[EXIT-NOVACUOUS] a run that did not complete MUST NOT report success")

    (match s
      ((Complete) 0)
      ((Stopped)  2)
      ((Failed)   3)
      ((Pending)  1)))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; State
  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------

  ;; The agent-invocation contract of proposal section 5, settled there as
  ;; "--agent-exe plus repeatable --agent-arg, with {prompt}/{out}/{workdir}
  ;; substituted per argument". exe, (args, (prompts-dir, timeout-seconds)).
  ;;
  ;; NOT a shell template. wasi.proc.run takes an executable and an argument
  ;; vector and does no shell interpretation, and passing the operator's
  ;; string to /bin/sh -c would restore shell semantics through a granted
  ;; binary and void the auditability the split delivers (section 5 item 1).
  ;;
  ;; NO ENV CHANNEL. wasi.proc.run has no env parameter, so LLMLL_CMD,
  ;; RFC_PIPELINE_PROMPT and RFC_PIPELINE_OUT are not exported; the two paths
  ;; reach the agent through argv (section 5 item 2, PROC-ENV-1).
  ;;
  ;; --prompts-dir has NO reference counterpart: PROMPTS is a module constant
  ;; the reference derives from __file__ (:81) and a compiled binary has no
  ;; __file__. It is required for the same reason --agent-cmd is, and the
  ;; added flag is disclosed rather than defaulted to a guess about the tree.
  (type AgentCfg (| AgentCfg (string, (list[string], (string, int)))))

  ;; workdir, (only-selection, (halt-at, (halt-kind, (forced, agent)))). The
  ;; selection travels as the raw comma-separated string rather than as a
  ;; list, because an empty string has to mean "every stage" and an empty list
  ;; would mean "no stage": string-split with an empty subject yields [""],
  ;; measured.
  (type Cfg (| Cfg (string, (string, (string, (string, (bool, AgentCfg)))))))

  ;; The run-common record of proposal section 4: config, the live manifest,
  ;; and the selected stage indices in registry order.
  (type Run (| Run (Cfg, (Json, list[int]))))

  ;; Proposal section 4's sum over phases. Each arm names the RESPONSE it is
  ;; about to receive, since the console loop delivers the response to the
  ;; command issued on entry to the arm.
  ;;
  ;;   Boot 0     r is the argument vector
  ;;   Boot 1     r is the MANIFEST.json read
  ;;   Digest     r is wasi.fs.sha256 of declared artifact j, on the resume probe
  ;;   Started    r is the clock reading taken when the stage started
  ;;   Made       r is the write of artifact j
  ;;   Summed     r is wasi.fs.sha256 of artifact j, for the outputs map
  ;;   Stamped    r is the clock reading taken at record time
  ;;   Recorded   r is the manifest write
  ;;   Ending     r is the last report; the next state is terminal
  ;;   Done       terminal; :status reads the code out of this arm
  ;;
  ;; Ending exists because of RC-4: done? is evaluated on the state a step
  ;; RETURNS and the terminating step's command is DROPPED, so a state that
  ;; must still say something cannot be terminal. Recorded needs no such arm,
  ;; the manifest write having already been performed on entry to it.
  ;;
  ;; The six 4b arms are the ported stage body, and they all carry the SAME
  ;; payload type. Six arms with six differently-shaped tuples is six
  ;; projection sites where proposal section 13 risk 6 bites (a wrong
  ;; projection over a pair chain TYPECHECKS); one Body type is one accessor
  ;; set to get right.
  ;;
  ;;   Pre        r is the read of the stage's precondition artifact
  ;;   Srcs       r is the listing of 00-source
  ;;   Src        r is the read of pinned source `idx`
  ;;   Tmpl       r is the read of the prompt template
  ;;   Delegate   r is wasi.proc.run's exit status
  ;;   Outp       r is the read of the declared output
  ;;
  ;; k, (start, (pre-text, (idx, (source-names, accumulated-source-text)))).
  (type Body (| Body (int, (int, (string, (int, (list[string], string)))))))

  (type Phase
    (| Boot     int)
    (| Digest   (int, (int, (bool, (bool, string)))))
    (| Started  int)
    (| Made     (int, (int, int)))
    (| Pre      Body)
    (| Srcs     Body)
    (| Src      Body)
    (| Tmpl     Body)
    (| Delegate Body)
    (| Outp     Body)
    (| Summed   (int, (int, (int, Json))))
    (| Stamped  (int, (int, Json)))
    (| Recorded (int, int))
    (| Ending   int)
    (| Done     int))

  ;; ---- accessors, written once ---------------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; Proposal section 13 risk 6: a wrong projection over a pair chain
  ;; TYPECHECKS. Every projection in this module is in one of the functions
  ;; below and no use site writes a bare (first (second ...)).

  (def-shell cfg-workdir [c: Cfg] -> string
    (match c ((Cfg p) (first p))))
  (def-shell cfg-only [c: Cfg] -> string
    (match c ((Cfg p) (first (second p)))))
  (def-shell cfg-halt-at [c: Cfg] -> string
    (match c ((Cfg p) (first (second (second p))))))
  (def-shell cfg-halt-kind [c: Cfg] -> string
    (match c ((Cfg p) (first (second (second (second p)))))))
  (def-shell cfg-forced [c: Cfg] -> bool
    (match c ((Cfg p) (first (second (second (second (second p))))))))
  (def-shell cfg-agent [c: Cfg] -> AgentCfg
    (match c ((Cfg p) (second (second (second (second (second p))))))))

  (def-shell ag-exe [a: AgentCfg] -> string
    (match a ((AgentCfg q) (first q))))
  (def-shell ag-args [a: AgentCfg] -> list[string]
    (match a ((AgentCfg q) (first (second q)))))
  (def-shell ag-prompts [a: AgentCfg] -> string
    (match a ((AgentCfg q) (first (second (second q))))))
  (def-shell ag-timeout [a: AgentCfg] -> int
    (match a ((AgentCfg q) (second (second (second q))))))

  (def-shell cfg-of [rn: Run] -> Cfg
    (match rn ((Run p) (first p))))
  (def-shell man-of [rn: Run] -> Json
    (match rn ((Run p) (first (second p)))))
  (def-shell sel-of [rn: Run] -> list[int]
    (match rn ((Run p) (second (second p)))))

  (def-shell with-cfg [rn: Run c: Cfg] -> Run
    (Run (pair c (pair (man-of rn) (sel-of rn)))))
  (def-shell with-man [rn: Run m: Json] -> Run
    (Run (pair (cfg-of rn) (pair m (sel-of rn)))))
  (def-shell with-sel [rn: Run xs: list[int]] -> Run
    (Run (pair (cfg-of rn) (pair (man-of rn) xs))))

  (def-shell dg-k [d: (int, (int, (bool, (bool, string))))] -> int
    (first d))
  (def-shell dg-j [d: (int, (int, (bool, (bool, string))))] -> int
    (first (second d)))
  (def-shell dg-present [d: (int, (int, (bool, (bool, string))))] -> bool
    (first (second (second d))))
  (def-shell dg-match [d: (int, (int, (bool, (bool, string))))] -> bool
    (first (second (second (second d)))))
  (def-shell dg-names [d: (int, (int, (bool, (bool, string))))] -> string
    (second (second (second (second d)))))
  (def-shell mk-dg [k: int j: int present: bool matched: bool names: string] -> Phase
    (Digest (pair k (pair j (pair present (pair matched names))))))

  ;; The 4b body payload. Written once, per the note on the Phase type.
  (def-shell b-k [b: Body] -> int
    (match b ((Body p) (first p))))
  (def-shell b-start [b: Body] -> int
    (match b ((Body p) (first (second p)))))
  (def-shell b-pre [b: Body] -> string
    (match b ((Body p) (first (second (second p))))))
  (def-shell b-idx [b: Body] -> int
    (match b ((Body p) (first (second (second (second p)))))))
  (def-shell b-names [b: Body] -> list[string]
    (match b ((Body p) (first (second (second (second (second p))))))))
  (def-shell b-acc [b: Body] -> string
    (match b ((Body p) (second (second (second (second (second p))))))))

  (def-shell mk-body [k: int start: int pretext: string idx: int
                      names: list[string] acc: string] -> Body
    (Body (pair k (pair start (pair pretext (pair idx (pair names acc)))))))

  (def-shell b-with-pre [b: Body pretext: string] -> Body
    (mk-body (b-k b) (b-start b) pretext (b-idx b) (b-names b) (b-acc b)))
  (def-shell b-with-names [b: Body names: list[string]] -> Body
    (mk-body (b-k b) (b-start b) (b-pre b) 0 names (b-acc b)))
  (def-shell b-advance [b: Body acc: string] -> Body
    (mk-body (b-k b) (b-start b) (b-pre b) (+ (b-idx b) 1) (b-names b) acc))

  (def-shell md-k [d: (int, (int, int))] -> int (first d))
  (def-shell md-j [d: (int, (int, int))] -> int (first (second d)))
  (def-shell md-start [d: (int, (int, int))] -> int (second (second d)))
  (def-shell mk-md [k: int j: int start: int] -> Phase
    (Made (pair k (pair j start))))

  (def-shell sm-k [d: (int, (int, (int, Json)))] -> int (first d))
  (def-shell sm-j [d: (int, (int, (int, Json)))] -> int (first (second d)))
  (def-shell sm-start [d: (int, (int, (int, Json)))] -> int (first (second (second d))))
  (def-shell sm-outs [d: (int, (int, (int, Json)))] -> Json (second (second (second d))))
  (def-shell mk-sm [k: int j: int start: int outs: Json] -> Phase
    (Summed (pair k (pair j (pair start outs)))))

  (def-shell st-k [d: (int, (int, Json))] -> int (first d))
  (def-shell st-start [d: (int, (int, Json))] -> int (first (second d)))
  (def-shell st-outs [d: (int, (int, Json))] -> Json (second (second d)))
  (def-shell mk-st [k: int start: int outs: Json] -> Phase
    (Stamped (pair k (pair start outs))))

  (def-shell rc-k [d: (int, int)] -> int (first d))
  (def-shell rc-code [d: (int, int)] -> int (second d))
  (def-shell mk-rc [k: int code: int] -> Phase
    (Recorded (pair k code)))

  (def-shell run-of [s: (Run, Phase)] -> Run (first s))
  (def-shell phase-of [s: (Run, Phase)] -> Phase (second s))
  (def-shell go [rn: Run p: Phase c: Command] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (pair (pair rn p) c))

  ;; ---- argv ----------------------------------------------------------

  ;; The element following `f`, or "". One left-to-right fold carrying
  ;; (value-so-far, take-the-next-one); a repeated flag keeps the FIRST value,
  ;; which is the opposite of argparse and is stated because nothing enforces
  ;; it.
  (def-shell flag-value [as: list[string] f: string] -> string
    (first (list-fold as (pair "" false)
             (fn [acc: (string, bool) a: string]
               (if (second acc)
                   (pair a false)
                   (if (= a f) (pair (first acc) true) acc))))))

  ;; EVERY element following an occurrence of `f`, in order. --agent-arg is
  ;; repeatable (proposal section 5 item 1), so the FIRST-wins rule flag-value
  ;; states above is exactly wrong for it and a second fold is owed rather
  ;; than a reinterpretation of the first.
  (def-shell flag-values [as: list[string] f: string] -> list[string]
    (first (list-fold as (pair (list-empty) false)
             (fn [acc: (list[string], bool) a: string]
               (if (second acc)
                   (pair (list-append (first acc) a) false)
                   (if (= a f) (pair (first acc) true) acc))))))

  (def-shell dflt [s: string d: string] -> string
    (if (string-empty? s) d s))

  ;; 1800 is the reference's --timeout default (:1866). A value that does not
  ;; parse falls back to the default rather than halting, which is where this
  ;; differs from argparse's `type=int`; the flag is an operator's and a
  ;; malformed one is not a stage condition.
  (def-shell int-flag [as: list[string] f: string d: int] -> int
    (unwrap-or (string-to-int (flag-value as f)) d))

  (def-shell parse-agent [as: list[string]] -> AgentCfg
    (AgentCfg (pair (flag-value as "--agent-exe")
                    (pair (flag-values as "--agent-arg")
                          (pair (flag-value as "--prompts-dir")
                                (int-flag as "--timeout" 1800))))))

  (def-shell parse-cfg [as: list[string]] -> Cfg
    (Cfg (pair (dflt (flag-value as "--workdir") ".")
               (pair (flag-value as "--only")
                     (pair (flag-value as "--halt-at")
                           (pair (flag-value as "--halt-kind")
                                 (pair (list-contains as "--force")
                                       (parse-agent as))))))))

  ;; "" when the required flags are present. The reference makes --agent-cmd
  ;; required at argparse (:1888-1891) and exits 2 through ap.error, BEFORE
  ;; any stage exists, so no stage status attaches; the same is true here and
  ;; the exit code is the literal 2 on the corrupt-manifest path's reasoning.
  ;; Required unconditionally, as the reference requires --agent-cmd
  ;; unconditionally, and not only when a delegated stage is selected.
  (def-shell missing-flags [c: Cfg] -> string
    (if (string-empty? (ag-exe (cfg-agent c)))
        "--agent-exe is required (the executable each delegated stage runs)"
        (if (string-empty? (ag-prompts (cfg-agent c)))
            "--prompts-dir is required (the directory holding the stage prompt templates)"
            "")))

  (def-shell argv-of [r: Response] -> list[string]
    (match r
      ((RList as) as)
      ((RNone)    (list-empty))
      ((RText _)  (list-empty))
      ((RCode _)  (list-empty))
      ((RErr _)   (list-empty))))

  ;; MEASURED at v0.14.84, and the spec does not say which way round it goes:
  ;; string-split takes the SEPARATOR FIRST. LLMLL.md:2433 gives the type as
  ;; `string string -> list[string]` with no argument names, the generated
  ;; body is `string_split sep str` (CodegenHs.hs:359-366), and there is not
  ;; one call site in the tree to read it off. Getting it backwards splits the
  ;; separator by the subject and yields [","], which type-checks and passes
  ;; every check-time gate.
  (def-shell split-on [sep: string s: string] -> list[string]
    (string-split sep s))

  (def-shell key-selected? [c: Cfg i: int] -> bool
    (if (string-empty? (cfg-only c))
        true
        (list-contains (split-on "," (cfg-only c)) (stage-key i))))

  (def-shell sel-indices [c: Cfg] -> list[int]
    (list-filter (range 0 (stage-count)) (fn [i: int] (key-selected? c i))))

  (def-shell idx-at [rn: Run k: int] -> int
    (unwrap-or (list-nth (sel-of rn) k) 0))

  ;; ---- paths and stub bodies -----------------------------------------

  (def-shell manifest-path [c: Cfg] -> string
    (string-concat (cfg-workdir c) "/MANIFEST.json"))

  (def-shell art-path [c: Cfg i: int j: int] -> string
    (string-concat-many [(cfg-workdir c) "/" (stage-out i j)]))

  (def-shell art-dir [c: Cfg i: int j: int] -> string
    (string-concat-many [(cfg-workdir c) "/" (stage-out-dir i j)]))

  ;; No body for the thirteen stages 4b does not port. The content is
  ;; per-artifact so that two stubs never share a digest, which would make a
  ;; cross-stage digest confusion invisible.
  ;;
  ;; A `.json` artifact gets a JSON stub, NEW AT 4b and for a reason 4a could
  ;; not have. Stage B is now real and reads stage A's PROVENANCE.json through
  ;; json-parse; stage A is not ported (it needs HTTP-GET-1) and is not listed
  ;; in any of proposal section 9's sub-phase rows, so its stub is what a run
  ;; selecting A,B hands to stage B. With the flat text stub every such run
  ;; halted at B on "does not parse as JSON", which is a correct decision over
  ;; a wrong input rather than a decision about the pipeline. The distinctness
  ;; property is unchanged: the stage key and the artifact path are both still
  ;; in the body, so no two stubs share a digest.
  (def-shell json-artifact? [p: string] -> bool
    (let [(k (string-length p))]
      (and (> k 5) (= (string-slice p (- k 5) k) ".json"))))

  (def-shell stub-body [i: int j: int] -> string
    (if (json-artifact? (stage-out i j))
        (string-concat-many
          ["{\n \"driver-ll\": \"4a stub\",\n \"stage\": \"" (stage-key i)
           "\",\n \"artifact\": \"" (stage-out i j) "\"\n}\n"])
        (string-concat-many
          ["driver-ll 4a stub\nstage=" (stage-key i)
           "\nartifact=" (stage-out i j) "\n"])))

  ;; ---- 4b paths and string helpers -----------------------------------

  (def-shell wd-path [c: Cfg rel: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many [(cfg-workdir c) "/" rel]))

  ;; The agent's directory IS the directory of the stage's first declared
  ;; output, measured against the reference at every ported stage: B writes
  ;; 01-scope/scope.md and delegates in 01-scope (:575-578), C 02-rubric
  ;; (:590-592), I 08-prereg (:1060-1065).
  (def-shell agent-dir [c: Cfg i: int] -> string
    (art-dir c i 0))

  (def-shell prompt-file [c: Cfg i: int] -> string
    (string-concat (agent-dir c i) "/PROMPT.md"))

  (def-shell tmpl-file [c: Cfg i: int] -> string
    (string-concat-many [(ag-prompts (cfg-agent c)) "/" (stage-prompt i)]))

  ;; The last path segment. `stage-out` is workdir-relative and the reference's
  ;; messages name the BASENAME ("wrote no scope.md", :333).
  (def-shell base-of [p: string] -> string
    (let [(ps (split-on "/" p))]
      (unwrap-or (list-nth ps (- (list-length ps) 1)) p)))

  ;; Every occurrence of `needle` replaced by `repl`. There is no
  ;; string-replace builtin, so this is split-then-rejoin: the fold emits the
  ;; first part bare and every later part preceded by the replacement, which
  ;; is exactly `repl.join(parts)`.
  (def-shell replace-all [s: string needle: string repl: string] -> string
    (second (list-fold (split-on needle s) (pair true "")
              (fn [acc: (bool, string) p: string]
                (pair false
                      (if (first acc) p
                          (string-concat-many [(second acc) repl p])))))))

  ;; Python's f"{n:5d}", built by cases rather than by string-slice so the
  ;; half-open convention of one more builtin is not on the critical path.
  (def-shell pad5 [n: int] -> string
    (let [(s (int-to-string n))]
      (let [(k (string-length s))]
        (string-concat
          (if (>= k 5) ""
          (if (= k 4) " "
          (if (= k 3) "  "
          (if (= k 2) "   "
          (if (= k 1) "    " "")))))
          s))))

  ;; _sources_text's numbering (:626-627), and the reason it exists is quoted
  ;; there: extraction rows cite line spans and reconciliation matches on them,
  ;; so asking an agent to count lines itself is a silent source of mismatched
  ;; spans. Lines split on newline ONLY, `wc -l` semantics, because RFC page
  ;; breaks are form feeds and Python's splitlines() would break on them too.
  ;;
  ;; list-nth is O(i), so this is quadratic in the LINE COUNT (not in
  ;; characters). Measured cost is a list traversal per line; the alternative
  ;; is a fold that concatenates a growing string, which is quadratic in
  ;; CHARACTERS and materially worse on an RFC-sized input.
  (def-shell numbered-lines [raw: string] -> string
    (let [(ls (split-on "\n" raw))]
      (string-concat-many
        (list-map (range 0 (list-length ls))
          (fn [i: int]
            (string-concat-many
              [(if (= i 0) "" "\n") (pad5 (+ i 1)) "| "
               (unwrap-or (list-nth ls i) "")]))))))

  ;; `sorted((ctx.workdir / "00-source").glob("*.txt"))` (:624). wasi.fs.list
  ;; already sorts (CodegenHs.hs wasi_fs_list, and the sort is there so the
  ;; event log is machine-independent), so only the suffix test is owed.
  (def-shell txt? [n: string] -> bool
    (let [(k (string-length n))]
      (and (> k 4) (= (string-slice n (- k 4) k) ".txt"))))

  ;; BARRIERS (:87-96) rendered as `json.dumps(BARRIERS, indent=1)` produces.
  ;; Embedded as the rendered constant rather than rebuilt through
  ;; json-serialize: the reference's dict is a module constant in insertion
  ;; order and LLMLL's serializer neither guarantees that order nor writes
  ;; indent=1, so re-serializing would hand the agent a different string for
  ;; no gain. It reaches only the prompt and no abstraction-function field.
  (def-shell barriers-json [] -> string
    "{\n \"B1\": \"timing / liveness\",\n \"B2\": \"transport binding\",\n \"B3\": \"trace-level property\",\n \"B4\": \"opaque transform\",\n \"B5\": \"string structure\",\n \"B6\": \"superseded / deprecated\",\n \"B7\": \"entailed by the model or by a named sibling row\",\n \"B8\": \"outside any tool\"\n}")

  ;; AgentRunner's template expansion (:301-303), applied per ARGUMENT rather
  ;; than to one shell string (proposal section 5 item 1). {workdir} is the
  ;; AGENT's directory, which is what the reference passes: `workdir` there is
  ;; the first parameter of AgentRunner.run, i.e. ctx.d("01-scope").
  (def-shell subst-arg [a: string prompt: string out: string wd: string] -> string
    (replace-all (replace-all (replace-all a "{prompt}" prompt) "{out}" out)
                 "{workdir}" wd))

  ;; ---- response projections ------------------------------------------

  ;; "" for every arm but RText. wasi.fs.sha256 answers RText on a present path
  ;; and RErr on a missing one, measured; the other three arms are unreachable
  ;; and land on the same safe side. See [DISCLOSURE skip.may-skip] item 4.
  (def-shell hex-of [r: Response] -> string
    (match r
      ((RText t)  t)
      ((RErr _)   "")
      ((RNone)    "")
      ((RCode _)  "")
      ((RList _)  "")))

  (def-shell ns-of [r: Response] -> int
    (match r
      ((RCode n)  n)
      ((RText _)  0)
      ((RErr _)   0)
      ((RNone)    0)
      ((RList _)  0)))

  ;; Whole seconds. json-of-int is the only numeric injection, so the field
  ;; cannot carry the reference's one-decimal float; see manifest.llmll's
  ;; header. Negative is impossible from a monotonic clock and is clamped
  ;; anyway, since a negative duration in an artifact is worse than a zero.
  (def-shell secs-between [start: int end: int] -> int
    (if (< end start) 0 (/ (- end start) 1000000000)))

  ;; ---- the injector --------------------------------------------------

  (def-shell injected-kind [rn: Run k: int] -> string
    (if (= (cfg-halt-at (cfg-of rn)) (stage-key (idx-at rn k)))
        (cfg-halt-kind (cfg-of rn))
        ""))

  (def-shell halts-pre? [rn: Run k: int] -> bool
    (or (= (injected-kind rn k) "ConditionUnmet")
        (= (injected-kind rn k) "Errored")))

  (def-shell halts-post? [rn: Run k: int] -> bool
    (= (injected-kind rn k) "PartialThenHalt"))

  (def-shell injected-outcome [rn: Run k: int] -> Outcome
    (if (= (injected-kind rn k) "ConditionUnmet") ConditionUnmet
    (if (= (injected-kind rn k) "PartialThenHalt") PartialThenHalt
        Errored)))

  (def-shell halt-detail [rn: Run k: int] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["injected halt at stage " (stage-key (idx-at rn k))
       " (--halt-kind " (injected-kind rn k) ")"]))

  ;; ConditionUnmet and PartialThenHalt are the stopped path and carry a
  ;; clause; Errored is the failed path and carries none, mirroring the
  ;; reference's two halt-row shapes.
  (def-shell halt-clause [o: Outcome] -> string
    (match o
      ((ConditionUnmet)  "4a-injector")
      ((PartialThenHalt) "4a-injector")
      ((Errored)         "")
      ((Finished)        "")))

  ;; ---- report lines --------------------------------------------------

  (def-shell skip-line [i: int] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["[driver-ll] stage " (stage-key i) " (" (stage-name i)
       "): already complete, skipping"]))

  ;; The reference computes `mismatched` under `if recorded and artifacts`
  ;; (:1934), so a stage with a missing declared artifact reports NOTHING and
  ;; re-runs silently. That is transition T7, it is conformant (section 5
  ;; attaches a MUST-report to T6 and a SHOULD to T8 and is silent here), and
  ;; the same gate is written here rather than inherited by accident.
  (def-shell reason-line [i: int recorded: bool present: bool names: string] -> string
    (if (and recorded (and present (not (string-empty? names))))
        (string-concat-many
          ["[driver-ll] stage " (stage-key i)
           ": artifact(s) changed since this stage recorded completion ("
           names "); re-running. An artifact modified outside the protocol is "
           "not that stage's output.\n"])
        (if (and present (not recorded))
            (string-concat-many
              ["[driver-ll] stage " (stage-key i)
               ": artifacts present but no completion record (interrupted, or "
               "a previous attempt failed); re-running\n"])
            "")))

  (def-shell run-lines [i: int recorded: bool present: bool names: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      [(reason-line i recorded present names)
       "[driver-ll] stage " (stage-key i) " [" (stage-kind i) "] " (stage-name i)]))

  (def-shell halt-line [st: Status i: int detail: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["[driver-ll] " (if (= st Failed) "FAILED" "STOP") " at stage "
       (stage-key i) ": " detail]))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; The step machine
  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------

  ;; Issue stage k's first digest probe, or finish the run. Every site that
  ;; would advance to the next stage calls this, so "past the last stage" is
  ;; decided in one place.
  (def-shell enter [rn: Run k: int] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (if (>= k (list-length (sel-of rn)))
        (go rn (Ending 0)
            (wasi.io.stdout
              (string-concat "[driver-ll] done. manifest: " (manifest-path (cfg-of rn)))))
        (go rn (mk-dg k 0 true true "")
            (wasi.fs.sha256 (art-path (cfg-of rn) (idx-at rn k) 0)))))

  (def-shell start-run [rn: Run] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (enter (with-sel rn (sel-indices (cfg-of rn))) 0))

  ;; An ABSENT manifest is an empty one, not a fault: the reference returns
  ;; {"stages": {}} when the path does not exist (:246-247). Only a manifest
  ;; that IS there and cannot be used halts.
  (def-shell boot-read [rn: Run r: Response] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (match r
      ((RText body)
        (if (string-empty? (manifest-fault body))
            (start-run (with-man rn (manifest-of body)))
            (go rn (Ending 2)
                (wasi.io.stdout
                  (string-concat "[driver-ll] STOP: " (manifest-fault body))))))
      ((RErr _)   (start-run (with-man rn (empty-manifest))))
      ((RNone)    (start-run (with-man rn (empty-manifest))))
      ((RCode _)  (start-run (with-man rn (empty-manifest))))
      ((RList _)  (start-run (with-man rn (empty-manifest))))))

  (def-shell boot-step [rn: Run n: int r: Response] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (if (= n 0)
        (let [(c (parse-cfg (argv-of r)))]
          (if (string-empty? (missing-flags c))
              (go (with-cfg rn c) (Boot 1)
                  (seq-commands (wasi.fs.mkdir (cfg-workdir c))
                                (wasi.fs.read (manifest-path c))))
              ;; Before any stage exists, so no Status attaches and
              ;; record-outcome is not called: the same reasoning the
              ;; corrupt-manifest path carries, and the same literal 2.
              (go (with-cfg rn c) (Ending 2)
                  (wasi.io.stdout
                    (string-concat "[driver-ll] STOP: " (missing-flags c))))))
        (boot-read rn r)))

  ;; The resume decision. Nothing here re-implements it: the four booleans go
  ;; to skip.may-skip and its answer is the branch.
  ;;
  ;; `digests-match` IS THE REFERENCE'S `not mismatched`, AND THE GATE ON IT IS
  ;; NOT DECORATION. The reference computes `mismatched` under
  ;; `if recorded and artifacts` (:1934), so when a declared artifact is absent
  ;; the list is EMPTY and the skip is prevented by condition (b) alone. The
  ;; accumulated `matched` below cannot say that on its own: an absent artifact
  ;; answers RErr, hex is "", and "" differs from the recorded digest, so
  ;; presence and match fail TOGETHER and (c) masks (b).
  ;;
  ;; MEASURED, and this is why the gate is here rather than left implicit. With
  ;; the raw `matched`, passing a hardcoded `true` for artifacts-present left
  ;; ALL FIFTEEN cover scenarios green, T7 included: the cell proposal section
  ;; 2.3 defines as "condition (b) alone" was being decided by (c). With the
  ;; gate, the same perturbation turns T7 red. The decision is unchanged either
  ;; way, [S5-SKIP] holding in both, so this is about what the cover can
  ;; OBSERVE rather than about what the driver does.
  (def-shell decide [rn: Run k: int present: bool matched: bool names: string]
      -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (let [(i (idx-at rn k))]
      (let [(recorded (= (row-status (man-of rn) (stage-key i)) "complete"))]
        (if (may-skip recorded present
                      (if (and recorded present) matched true)
                      (cfg-forced (cfg-of rn)))
            (let [(e (enter rn (+ k 1)))]
              (pair (first e)
                    (seq-commands (wasi.io.stdout (skip-line i)) (second e))))
            (go rn (Started k)
                (seq-commands
                  (wasi.io.stdout (run-lines i recorded present names))
                  wasi.clock.monotonic))))))

  (def-shell digest-step [rn: Run d: (int, (int, (bool, (bool, string)))) r: Response]
      -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (let [(i (idx-at rn (dg-k d)))]
      (let [(hex (hex-of r))]
        (let [(want (recorded-digest (man-of rn) (stage-key i) (stage-out i (dg-j d))))]
          (let [(here (not (string-empty? hex)))]
            (let [(same (and here (= hex want)))]
              (let [(names (if same
                               (dg-names d)
                               (if here
                                   (string-concat-many
                                     [(dg-names d)
                                      (if (string-empty? (dg-names d)) "" ", ")
                                      (stage-out i (dg-j d))])
                                   (dg-names d))))]
                (if (< (+ (dg-j d) 1) (stage-out-count i))
                    (go rn (mk-dg (dg-k d) (+ (dg-j d) 1)
                                  (and (dg-present d) here)
                                  (and (dg-match d) same)
                                  names)
                        (wasi.fs.sha256 (art-path (cfg-of rn) i (+ (dg-j d) 1))))
                    (decide rn (dg-k d)
                            (and (dg-present d) here)
                            (and (dg-match d) same)
                            names)))))))))

  ;; The manifest write and the halt report leave in one seq-commands, so the
  ;; response the next arm receives is the WRITE's. RC-2 is discard-left.
  ;;
  ;; ONE HALT SITE. Every halt in this module reaches record-outcome here and
  ;; nowhere else, so the Outcome-to-Status mapping is proved once for the 4a
  ;; injector and for all nine 4b stage-body conditions alike.
  (def-shell halt-with [rn: Run k: int o: Outcome detail: string clause: string]
      -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (let [(i (idx-at rn k))]
      (let [(st (record-outcome o))]
        (let [(m2 (put-row (man-of rn) (stage-key i)
                           (halt-row st o detail clause)))]
          (go (with-man rn m2) (mk-rc k (exit-code st))
              (seq-commands
                (wasi.io.stdout (halt-line st i detail))
                (wasi.fs.write (manifest-path (cfg-of rn)) (json-serialize m2))))))))

  (def-shell halt-now [rn: Run k: int o: Outcome start: int] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (halt-with rn k o (halt-detail rn k) (halt-clause o)))

  ;; THE 4b HALT CHANNEL, and the reason validate.llmll is a module rather
  ;; than three copies of an if. A Verdict goes to verdict-outcome and the
  ;; Outcome it returns goes to record-outcome; the clause is "" because
  ;; [V7-NO-STOP] and [V7-NO-PARTIAL] PROVE the result is not a stopped
  ;; constructor, so there is no clause to cite and nothing here decides that.
  (def-shell halt-verdict [rn: Run k: int v: Verdict detail: string]
      -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (halt-with rn k (verdict-outcome v) detail ""))

  ;; The two AgentRunner conditions that are NOT facts about an output's
  ;; shape: a budget overrun and a non-zero exit. See the header's statement 3
  ;; for why they do not go through a Verdict.
  (def-shell halt-errored [rn: Run k: int detail: string] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (halt-with rn k Errored detail ""))

  (def-shell write-cmd [rn: Run k: int j: int] -> Command
    (seq-commands (wasi.fs.mkdir (art-dir (cfg-of rn) (idx-at rn k) j))
                  (wasi.fs.write (art-path (cfg-of rn) (idx-at rn k) j)
                                 (stub-body (idx-at rn k) j))))

  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;; 4b: the ported body of a delegated stage.
  ;;
  ;; The ORDER reproduces the reference's, and the order is what decides
  ;; WHICH condition a broken workdir reports. Stage B reads PROVENANCE.json
  ;; (:573) before it reads the pinned sources (:574), so a workdir missing
  ;; both names the provenance; reversing them would still record `failed` and
  ;; would name the wrong cause, which is the difference section 4:129-131's
  ;; detail requirement is there to keep.
  ;;
  ;;   B   Pre(PROVENANCE.json, must parse) -> Srcs -> Src* -> Tmpl
  ;;         -> Delegate -> Outp(floor 200)
  ;;   C   Srcs -> Src* -> Tmpl -> Delegate -> Outp(floor 400)
  ;;   I   Pre(01-scope/scope.md, presence only) -> Tmpl -> Delegate
  ;;         -> Outp(floor -1, which passes every present output)
  ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------

  (def-shell to-srcs [rn: Run b: Body] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (go rn (Srcs b) (wasi.fs.list (wd-path (cfg-of rn) "00-source"))))

  (def-shell to-tmpl [rn: Run b: Body] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (go rn (Tmpl b)
        (wasi.fs.read (tmpl-file (cfg-of rn) (idx-at rn (b-k b))))))

  (def-shell after-pre [rn: Run b: Body] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (if (stage-needs-src? (idx-at rn (b-k b)))
        (to-srcs rn b)
        (to-tmpl rn b)))

  (def-shell fresh-body [k: int start: int] -> Body
    (mk-body k start "" 0 (list-empty) ""))

  ;; A stage with no precondition artifact goes straight past Pre.
  (def-shell begin-body [rn: Run k: int start: int] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (let [(i (idx-at rn k))]
      (if (string-empty? (stage-pre-path i 0))
          (after-pre rn (fresh-body k start))
          (go rn (Pre (fresh-body k start))
              (wasi.fs.read (wd-path (cfg-of rn) (stage-pre-path i 0)))))))

  (def-shell started-step [rn: Run k: int r: Response] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (if (halts-pre? rn k)
        (halt-now rn k (injected-outcome rn k) (ns-of r))
        (if (stage-ported? (idx-at rn k))
            (begin-body rn k (ns-of r))
            (go rn (mk-md k 0 (ns-of r)) (write-cmd rn k 0)))))

  ;; ---- Pre -------------------------------------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; The two guarded reads of proposal section 9.1 item 1. Absent covers a
  ;; path that is not there and one whose bytes do not decode; Malformed is
  ;; stage B's parse, which the reference performs with read_json and which
  ;; therefore fails there too, as a traceback rather than as a decision.

  (def-shell pre-detail [i: int what: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["stage " (stage-key i) ": " (stage-pre-path i 0) " " what
       ". The stage cannot be delegated without it; run the stage that "
       "produces it, or delete its manifest row to re-run."]))

  (def-shell pre-step [rn: Run b: Body r: Response] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (let [(i (idx-at rn (b-k b)))]
      (match r
        ((RText t)
          (if (and (= (stage-pre-mode i 0) 1) (not (is-ok (json-parse t))))
              (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Malformed
                            (pre-detail i "does not parse as JSON"))
              (after-pre rn (b-with-pre b t))))
        ((RErr _)  (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Absent
                                 (pre-detail i "is absent or is not readable text")))
        ((RNone)   (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Absent (pre-detail i "was not readable")))
        ((RCode _) (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Absent (pre-detail i "was not readable")))
        ((RList _) (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Absent (pre-detail i "was not readable"))))))

  ;; ---- Srcs and Src ----------------------------------------------------

  (def-shell names-of [r: Response] -> list[string]
    (match r
      ((RList xs) (list-filter xs (fn [n: string] (txt? n))))
      ((RText _)  (list-empty))
      ((RErr _)   (list-empty))
      ((RNone)    (list-empty))
      ((RCode _)  (list-empty))))

  (def-shell src-path [c: Cfg n: string] -> string
    (wd-path c (string-concat "00-source/" n)))

  ;; `require(parts, "no pinned RFC text found; run stage A first")` (:629),
  ;; verbatim. An RErr from the listing (no 00-source directory at all) lands
  ;; on the same condition, which is what the reference's empty glob does.
  (def-shell srcs-step [rn: Run b: Body r: Response] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (let [(ns (names-of r))]
      (if (= (list-length ns) 0)
          (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Absent
                        "no pinned RFC text found; run stage A first")
          (go rn (Src (b-with-names b ns))
              (wasi.fs.read (src-path (cfg-of rn) (unwrap-or (list-nth ns 0) "")))))))

  (def-shell src-piece [name: string raw: string leading: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      [leading "===== " name " =====\n" (numbered-lines raw)]))

  (def-shell src-name [b: Body] -> string
    (unwrap-or (list-nth (b-names b) (b-idx b)) ""))

  (def-shell src-next [rn: Run b2: Body] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (if (< (b-idx b2) (list-length (b-names b2)))
        (go rn (Src b2) (wasi.fs.read (src-path (cfg-of rn) (src-name b2))))
        (to-tmpl rn b2)))

  (def-shell src-step [rn: Run b: Body r: Response] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (match r
      ((RText t)
        (src-next rn (b-advance b (string-concat (b-acc b)
                       (src-piece (src-name b) t
                                  (if (= (b-idx b) 0) "" "\n\n"))))))
      ((RErr e)  (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Absent
                   (string-concat-many
                     ["pinned source " (src-name b) " is not readable text ("
                      e "). The reference reads it with errors=\"replace\" and "
                      "cannot reach this; the port has no such channel and "
                      "records the stage failed rather than substituting bytes "
                      "no agent can cite."])))
      ((RNone)   (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Absent "pinned source unreadable"))
      ((RCode _) (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Absent "pinned source unreadable"))
      ((RList _) (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Absent "pinned source unreadable"))))

  ;; ---- Tmpl ------------------------------------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; Ctx.prompt (:535-541): substitute each keyword, then reject any
  ;; placeholder left behind. The substitution ORDER is the reference's
  ;; keyword order, because a value carrying a later placeholder would be
  ;; expanded by it: rfc_text then provenance for B (:576-577), rfc_text for C
  ;; (:591), scope then barriers for I (:1062-1064).

  (def-shell render [i: int tpl: string src: string pretext: string] -> string
    (let [(t1 (if (stage-needs-src? i) (replace-all tpl "{{rfc_text}}" src) tpl))]
      (let [(t2 (if (string-empty? (stage-pre-key i 0))
                    t1
                    (replace-all t1 (stage-pre-key i 0) pretext)))]
        (if (stage-needs-barriers? i)
            (replace-all t2 "{{barriers}}" (barriers-json))
            t2))))

  ;; The prompt write, then the agent, in one seq-commands. RC-2 is
  ;; discard-left, so the response the Delegate arm receives is the RUN's and
  ;; the write's is dropped -- which mirrors the reference, whose
  ;; prompt_path.write_text (:299) is likewise unchecked.
  (def-shell delegate-cmd [rn: Run i: int rendered: string] -> Command
    (let [(c (cfg-of rn))]
      (let [(dir (agent-dir c i))]
        (seq-commands
          (wasi.fs.mkdir dir)
          (seq-commands
            (wasi.fs.write (prompt-file c i) rendered)
            (wasi.proc.run
              (ag-exe (cfg-agent c))
              (list-map (ag-args (cfg-agent c))
                (fn [a: string] (subst-arg a (prompt-file c i)
                                             (art-path c i 0) dir)))
              dir
              (string-concat dir "/agent.stdout.log")
              (string-concat dir "/agent.stderr.log")
              (ag-timeout (cfg-agent c))))))))

  (def-shell tmpl-step [rn: Run b: Body r: Response] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (let [(i (idx-at rn (b-k b)))]
      (match r
        ((RText tpl)
          (let [(rendered (render i tpl (b-acc b) (b-pre b)))]
            (if (string-contains rendered "{{")
                (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Malformed
                  (string-concat-many
                    ["prompt " (stage-prompt i) ": unfilled placeholders remain "
                     "after substitution"]))
                (go rn (Delegate b) (delegate-cmd rn i rendered)))))
        ((RErr _)  (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Absent
                     (string-concat-many
                       ["prompt " (stage-prompt i) ": not readable under "
                        "--prompts-dir " (ag-prompts (cfg-agent (cfg-of rn)))])))
        ((RNone)   (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Absent "prompt template unreadable"))
        ((RCode _) (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Absent "prompt template unreadable"))
        ((RList _) (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) Absent "prompt template unreadable")))))

  ;; ---- Delegate --------------------------------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; AgentRunner.run's two non-output conditions. RErr carries BOTH the budget
  ;; overrun (:317-326) and a spawn that never started, which the reference
  ;; would see as exit 127 from the shell; the port cannot tell them apart at
  ;; the response and says so in the detail rather than picking one.
  ;;
  ;; Rev 9 settles what section 3.1's row 3 and section 10 case 4 do not say:
  ;; the non-zero-exit raise at :328-330 runs BEFORE the output-existence
  ;; check at :331-334, so a stage that produced a valid declared output and
  ;; exited non-zero records `failed`, not `complete`. Measured; see the 4b
  ;; plan. This arm reproduces the reference and not the proposal's table.

  (def-shell delegate-step [rn: Run b: Body r: Response] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (let [(i (idx-at rn (b-k b)))]
      (match r
        ((RCode c)
          (if (= c 0)
              (go rn (Outp b) (wasi.fs.read (art-path (cfg-of rn) i 0)))
              (halt-errored rn (b-k b)
                (string-concat-many
                  ["agent[" (stage-agent-label i 0) "] exited " (int-to-string c)
                   "; see " (agent-dir (cfg-of rn) i) "/agent.stderr.log"]))))
        ((RErr e)
          (halt-errored rn (b-k b)
            (string-concat-many
              ["agent[" (stage-agent-label i 0) "] did not complete within its "
               (int-to-string (ag-timeout (cfg-agent (cfg-of rn))))
               "s budget, or could not be started (" e "). Its partial work is "
               "in " (agent-dir (cfg-of rn) i) ". Re-run this stage with a "
               "larger --timeout, or treat the overrun as a finding."])))
        ((RText _) (halt-errored rn (b-k b) "agent produced no exit status"))
        ((RNone)   (halt-errored rn (b-k b) "agent produced no exit status"))
        ((RList _) (halt-errored rn (b-k b) "agent produced no exit status")))))

  ;; ---- Outp ------------------------------------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; THE ACCEPTANCE CLAUSE OF SUB-PHASE 4b LANDS HERE. `present` and `size`
  ;; are handed to validate.verdict-of together with the floor the STAGE
  ;; CONTRACT declares, and the Verdict is handed to verdict-outcome. Nothing
  ;; in this function decides what a failing output does; it decides what the
  ;; operator is told it was.

  (def-shell floor-detail [i: int] -> string
    (if (= i 1)
        "stage B: scope.md is too short to state a boundary"
        (if (= i 2)
            "stage C: rubric.md is too short"
            (string-concat-many
              ["stage " (stage-key i) ": " (base-of (stage-out i 0))
               " is below the floor its stage contract declares"]))))

  (def-shell absent-detail [c: Cfg i: int] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["agent[" (stage-agent-label i 0) "] exited 0 but wrote no "
       (base-of (stage-out i 0)) "; the stage contract was not met (see "
       (agent-dir c i) "/agent.stdout.log)"]))

  (def-shell outp-detail [c: Cfg i: int v: Verdict] -> string
    (match v
      ((Absent)    (absent-detail c i))
      ((Malformed) (floor-detail i))
      ((Passed)    "")))

  (def-shell outp-decide [rn: Run b: Body present: bool size: int]
      -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (let [(i (idx-at rn (b-k b)))]
      (let [(v (verdict-of present size (stage-floor i)))]
        (match v
          ((Passed)
            (go rn (mk-sm (b-k b) 0 (b-start b) json-object)
                (wasi.fs.sha256 (art-path (cfg-of rn) i 0))))
          ((Absent)    (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) v (outp-detail (cfg-of rn) i v)))
          ((Malformed) (halt-verdict rn (b-k b) v (outp-detail (cfg-of rn) i v)))))))

  (def-shell outp-step [rn: Run b: Body r: Response] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (match r
      ((RText t)  (outp-decide rn b true (string-length t)))
      ((RErr _)   (outp-decide rn b false 0))
      ((RNone)    (outp-decide rn b false 0))
      ((RCode _)  (outp-decide rn b false 0))
      ((RList _)  (outp-decide rn b false 0))))

  (def-shell made-step [rn: Run d: (int, (int, int)) r: Response] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (if (< (+ (md-j d) 1) (stage-out-count (idx-at rn (md-k d))))
        (go rn (mk-md (md-k d) (+ (md-j d) 1) (md-start d))
            (write-cmd rn (md-k d) (+ (md-j d) 1)))
        (go rn (mk-sm (md-k d) 0 (md-start d) json-object)
            (wasi.fs.sha256 (art-path (cfg-of rn) (idx-at rn (md-k d)) 0)))))

  ;; The reference's outputs map is comprehended over the declared outputs THAT
  ;; EXIST (:2010-2011), so a missing artifact yields a SHORT map rather than a
  ;; null. An RErr here is that filter.
  (def-shell summed-step [rn: Run d: (int, (int, (int, Json))) r: Response]
      -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (let [(i (idx-at rn (sm-k d)))]
      (let [(outs (if (string-empty? (hex-of r))
                      (sm-outs d)
                      (jset (sm-outs d) (stage-out i (sm-j d))
                            (json-of-string (hex-of r)))))]
        (if (< (+ (sm-j d) 1) (stage-out-count i))
            (go rn (mk-sm (sm-k d) (+ (sm-j d) 1) (sm-start d) outs)
                (wasi.fs.sha256 (art-path (cfg-of rn) i (+ (sm-j d) 1))))
            (go rn (mk-st (sm-k d) (sm-start d) outs) wasi.clock.monotonic)))))

  (def-shell stamped-step [rn: Run d: (int, (int, Json)) r: Response]
      -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (if (halts-post? rn (st-k d))
        (halt-now rn (st-k d) PartialThenHalt (st-start d))
        (let [(i (idx-at rn (st-k d)))]
          (let [(m2 (put-row (man-of rn) (stage-key i)
                             (complete-row (stage-kind i)
                                           (secs-between (st-start d) (ns-of r))
                                           (st-outs d))))]
            (go (with-man rn m2) (mk-rc (st-k d) 0)
                (wasi.fs.write (manifest-path (cfg-of rn)) (json-serialize m2)))))))

  ;; The manifest write has already happened on entry here, so a halt can go
  ;; straight to Done: the command this arm returns is the one RC-4 drops.
  (def-shell recorded-step [rn: Run d: (int, int)] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (if (= (rc-code d) 0)
        (enter rn (+ (rc-k d) 1))
        (go rn (Done (rc-code d)) (wasi.io.stdout ""))))

  (def-shell drv-init [] -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (pair (pair (Run (pair (Cfg (pair "."
                                 (pair "" (pair "" (pair ""
                                   (pair false
                                     (AgentCfg (pair "" (pair (list-empty)
                                                        (pair "" 1800))))))))))
                           (pair (empty-manifest) (list-empty))))
                (Boot 0))
          wasi.proc.args))

  (def-shell drv-step [s: (Run, Phase) input: string r: Response]
      -> ((Run, Phase), Command)
    (match (phase-of s)
      ((Boot n)     (boot-step   (run-of s) n r))
      ((Digest d)   (digest-step (run-of s) d r))
      ((Started k)  (started-step (run-of s) k r))
      ((Made d)     (made-step   (run-of s) d r))
      ((Pre b)      (pre-step    (run-of s) b r))
      ((Srcs b)     (srcs-step   (run-of s) b r))
      ((Src b)      (src-step    (run-of s) b r))
      ((Tmpl b)     (tmpl-step   (run-of s) b r))
      ((Delegate b) (delegate-step (run-of s) b r))
      ((Outp b)     (outp-step   (run-of s) b r))
      ((Summed d)   (summed-step (run-of s) d r))
      ((Stamped d)  (stamped-step (run-of s) d r))
      ((Recorded d) (recorded-step (run-of s) d))
      ((Ending c)   (go (run-of s) (Done c) (wasi.io.stdout "")))
      ((Done c)     (go (run-of s) (Done c) (wasi.io.stdout "")))))

  (def-shell drv-done? [s: (Run, Phase)] -> bool
    (match (phase-of s)
      ((Boot _)     false)
      ((Digest _)   false)
      ((Started _)  false)
      ((Made _)     false)
      ((Pre _)      false)
      ((Srcs _)     false)
      ((Src _)      false)
      ((Tmpl _)     false)
      ((Delegate _) false)
      ((Outp _)     false)
      ((Summed _)   false)
      ((Stamped _)  false)
      ((Recorded _) false)
      ((Ending _)   false)
      ((Done _)     true)))

  (def-shell done-code [s: (Run, Phase)] -> int
    (match (phase-of s)
      ((Boot _)     1)
      ((Digest _)   1)
      ((Started _)  1)
      ((Made _)     1)
      ((Pre _)      1)
      ((Srcs _)     1)
      ((Src _)      1)
      ((Tmpl _)     1)
      ((Delegate _) 1)
      ((Outp _)     1)
      ((Summed _)   1)
      ((Stamped _)  1)
      ((Recorded _) 1)
      ((Ending _)   1)
      ((Done c)     c)))

  ;; The terminal marker. It leaves through :on-done rather than through a
  ;; step, because RC-4 drops the terminating step's command and a halt report
  ;; that is constructed and not performed fails driver-spec section 4:139-143
  ;; SILENTLY. spine.llmll:673-679 records :on-done as unusable and is right
  ;; for an int state; section 4's sum carries the reason, so it becomes
  ;; available here.
  (def-shell drv-final [s: (Run, Phase)] -> Command
    (wasi.io.stdout
      (string-concat "[driver-ll] end status=" (int-to-string (done-code s)))))

  ;; :status. Contract-checked, not body-faithful; see the header. The clamp is
  ;; what makes the declared range true by construction rather than by
  ;; assertion, and it fails in the safe direction: 256 exits 255, not 0.
  ;;
  ;; EVERY ARM BINDS ITS PAYLOAD, including the eight that ignore it. A `_`
  ;; there is PWildcard, and isCoreBodySyntactic's payload-constructor leg
  ;; (Syntax.hs:810) admits only PConstructor n [PVar _], so a wildcard turns
  ;; the whole match into non-core syntax and the def is rejected at `check`.
  (def drv-status [s: (Run, Phase)] -> int
    (post (and (>= result 0) (<= result 255))
      :source "[EXIT-RANGE] PROC-BOUNDARY-1 sec 4.4 - the declared range on the function :status names")
    (match (second s)
      ((Boot b)     1)
      ((Digest d)   1)
      ((Started k)  1)
      ((Made m)     1)
      ((Pre pb)     1)
      ((Srcs sb)    1)
      ((Src cb)     1)
      ((Tmpl tb)    1)
      ((Delegate g) 1)
      ((Outp ob)    1)
      ((Summed u)   1)
      ((Stamped t)  1)
      ((Recorded v) 1)
      ((Ending e)   1)
      ((Done c)     (if (< c 0) 1 (if (> c 255) 255 c)))))

  (def-main
    :mode    console
    :init    (drv-init)
    :step    drv-step
    :done?   drv-done?
    :on-done drv-final
    :status  drv-status))
